#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503817} :activity/id #uuid "1951ef34-d2da-547d-b92d-6f3e9b24507e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a22e35d-f540-59ba-9ba7-9af1c2046fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503003} {:answer/id #uuid "4be847b3-c2c0-563c-9815-5338039bfb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503124} {:answer/id #uuid "5a23779b-a5e0-5513-8d10-57ba249075f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503155} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad11e55-5175-51b9-9e8f-656a196e28ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503289} {:answer/id #uuid "bb74efbe-8eb8-51fd-9a34-c5b2d935c0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503289} :answer-field/id #uuid "1708397b-83ae-5c80-89f5-21129d6d07ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fed62691-6668-5c74-90ca-07c937644bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-72361" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+1}{g(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+x)^{′}}{(g(x)-x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)+1}{{g}^{′}(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+1}{{g}^{′}(x)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x)+1)^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x)-1)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=1,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{{g}^{″}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "0341db66-4a35-52c0-ad9b-5f93a0cf7225"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c242a0-360d-5c3f-a206-335a5f11f547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(1+2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503152} {:answer/id #uuid "6b102511-7ce6-5667-8a9e-af99d880f397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503191} {:answer/id #uuid "748328ef-0f70-5abe-be70-fdf38563a350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503215} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f56cd5-1bd4-5680-9418-30409996f34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503353} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1796f3-aef2-54bc-967e-df5e4e0f94d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503191} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c040818-e74b-5b00-9b27-984282da1f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4ce61c51-3f39-5f7e-96d5-a322aa4a1799" :question/math-academy-id "q-118923" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$6.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, what is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/41/417b284c87f735eb0c33f2369262b1548f9c54ff27ecbeb7880e91e9bc847f31.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =12+l+\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $12,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<12.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{144-{l}^{2}} & =l \\\\ 144-{l}^{2} & ={l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =144 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =72 \\\\ l & =6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,6\\sqrt{2})$ | $6\\sqrt{2}$ | $(6\\sqrt{2},12)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=6\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\sqrt{2}) & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{144-72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =12+6\\sqrt{2}+6\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12+12\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =12(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "053d77c3-8a49-5705-b184-df8c4f01cb7a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63746cd7-d78b-55d9-9dd3-7e1c9980ab08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503174} {:answer/id #uuid "8b920ce3-ff7b-5bb6-8fdf-35583cef9643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503263} {:answer/id #uuid "a0187cce-8b24-5027-bfa7-f2e2f49ae2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-5x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503301} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e1935b-7c69-571c-988f-1bd72cb529a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503311} {:answer/id #uuid "f352bcca-38a2-5ecb-8851-59eb03ee8d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503477} :answer-field/id #uuid "968ccea1-eddc-5df5-b531-eb97516921db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ac66977-fb7e-55ea-b01a-306cc1fa6e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138848" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "097cd705-286e-5518-8b5b-e2bba670d970"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9a8d86-2ffd-5e45-8af5-dbc5542af39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503012} {:answer/id #uuid "90c9d555-99b8-53e3-88c7-b225b4935425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503269} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cc92cf-53cb-5cf1-a4fe-3b86e44133f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503339} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a928d8-001f-5b4e-b2db-218ec5e4289a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}),\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503417} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd1915-0f33-5496-97c5-b477aee6adf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503012} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a59f508-2043-5fd3-9b05-0601035dc322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28cd517a-376e-5db1-863f-bc38045c2ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-138921" :question/problem "Find the real solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) & =-3 \\\\ 2({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =-3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-2 & =-3{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{x}-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-1)({e}^{x}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-1=0,\\,{e}^{x}+2=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-1 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}+2 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =-2 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) &  & \\, & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no real values of $x$ for which ${e}^{x}=-2.$ Therefore, $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})$ is the only real solution to the equation."} :step/id #uuid "0dd12746-614b-56bd-8219-38b7f64cc9df"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc45995-cc23-5f2f-95dc-adb53fd70390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503122} {:answer/id #uuid "7d02c22e-415b-580d-b404-ffc0a846300a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503238} {:answer/id #uuid "9b48a15c-7746-52d0-ab31-148949b28472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503291} {:answer/id #uuid "a689aded-b802-5144-9786-711cd58ea390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503317} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c13605-3b15-5441-9ad6-991d71ce870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503342} :answer-field/id #uuid "c41454b8-a54a-5924-9bf1-be03ac5b4d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55d1a1bd-c8eb-5ee7-88d6-a44ae7dc2c96" :question/math-academy-id "q-118710" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x-1)+1}{x-1})}^{3x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x-1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x}{x-1})}^{3x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x-1})}^{3(x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1031e6a3-2568-506d-9850-2de85ed63b35"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503769 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d635a92-3fd4-5667-81aa-6078f06bd72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503036} {:answer/id #uuid "920a8dbd-27b3-5123-881e-16a876ff9404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503271} {:answer/id #uuid "b986b8c3-93d6-5446-a4fe-c87752d0477c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503355} {:answer/id #uuid "df597881-80e1-518d-bee5-53858adff232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503436} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a063b0-76fe-550d-ae01-e8af9d24fad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503271} :answer-field/id #uuid "9482b22c-5636-533b-a174-f50d48546d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7e3d6fb3-96ca-598d-b899-4cfc00c51648" :question/math-academy-id "q-107412" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+2)(2x-1)}{(x-2)(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne \\pm 2,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=-\\frac{2x-1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=2.$ So statement II is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ | $2$ | $2.001$ | $2.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $298$ | $2\\,998$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-3\\,002$ | $-302$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 2$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 2$ from the right . Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e96d6a81813c1c89a498d0206234f2685f0a53c1bab626ce7430d84f9c0f506.png)"} :step/id #uuid "19b316f6-8f9d-5a8d-adf5-5812ef547f3c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4825a0cb-b3fb-5323-8a58-511f26bf6c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(y-2)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503116} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6a63f8-ed2d-5275-9398-6d460d70e710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4(x+1)}{y-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503196} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfcca81-3430-5271-8cd0-80e5d2035124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-2}{4(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503241} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c781b7-f9f3-553f-b0be-cf3d3b1d44f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503376} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b72652-cf0b-559e-99bc-226911189b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{2(y-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503376} :answer-field/id #uuid "88db5750-a4d8-5c4f-b26f-ef8a62130a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e5b95a8-8914-5efe-a3ab-4b5dce1c8cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-127521" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e74f96531b57a877b043ac4302adae6beef90dc24d64efc8e1a24b563e4738b7.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the ellipse shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since our ellipse is centered at $(-1,2)$ and has semiaxes $4$ and $2,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x+1)}{16}+\\frac{2(y-2)}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x+1}{4(y-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c57e98e-6773-5847-9f46-1df81e5dfb3c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60cd0cb8-cfc6-5e75-8468-3dd21d19f8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2c/2cebc6d4e2a93fac67aa135952f283ba2e5780ec920db28b4e9dc0a280ca2284.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503169} {:answer/id #uuid "9db8e3c3-8688-5ec4-8963-e4d97827cacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b1/b1b2ad9ee747fd3b33582d02e2a9e70de87027d9266ba8ac5b4b5c7f1fff696c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503296} {:answer/id #uuid "a63e6117-ba6b-5a37-b61a-9b783a96b0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b5c5ed1dc6a8597603258dae636334d91324f4636fbbdf18cb8b2c8a919c1e62.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503316} {:answer/id #uuid "c94e71de-8159-5a0b-84e3-25e2cb48b3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/78a1f253f739855621f6b615d3c0f558ad35e020e5d7216d2bd45aaa24ecc957.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503383} {:answer/id #uuid "fffeff41-bc47-5ab4-9e5b-8411914a2274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1e/1ecf03e6020276f37e6186c052d3463aa7b039806e204e018e938ab9896665fb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503514} :answer-field/id #uuid "f07b5cf1-c779-56d4-b26c-f37aa9c40d28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1508af35-4265-5067-b707-b4b8e57d8d0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-82180" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $f(x)$ that satisfies the conditions below?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ and ${f}^{″}(x)$ are undefined at $x=1$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\n- if $x\\in (-\\infty ,1)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ and if $x\\in (1,\\infty )$ then ${f}^{″}(x)>0$" :question/worked-solution "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘\\,\\,⌢$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗\\,\\,⌣$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=1$, where the derivative is undefined.\n\n- Since $x=1$ is a local minimum, the graph must go up to the left and to the right of that point.\n\n- The graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b08e02b0999da13ec71b2fee4e2df4a8754f54d99cb711e6ff19c7a81ace9de.png)"} :step/id #uuid "508ef64a-f293-50a8-aa88-8531f29af84e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045cd83b-c53b-5006-98d4-7381c08d15f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502987} {:answer/id #uuid "96818fa7-b929-56b1-8ae7-8ed6ae63e7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503277} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad9c93a-6f63-5b1a-a5cd-c09f666ff61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503290} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0a05f-0b07-5c7d-b39c-d245b3b01504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503410} {:answer/id #uuid "def8cad4-50ed-56ae-b280-b8ca407ceffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503432} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c05448-d8fc-508f-967b-41b75a786f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3cc6eed-e52f-5a0b-bc51-e9a47cb4eee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73427" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x+3)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$ only."} :step/id #uuid "52c44d60-05a7-554d-b9c2-75ab7b8cff31"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c714b33-004b-5d9e-9053-a9b7a733a681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503127} {:answer/id #uuid "6b66b271-9507-58e9-99e0-0fe7ede3639c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503193} {:answer/id #uuid "a5810297-ae4c-5cce-8442-330c69509540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503314} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d689b2-11ba-5108-abea-16bbe6fc4773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503354} {:answer/id #uuid "e2aa52af-fca4-533c-a616-407168a944a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503193} :answer-field/id #uuid "760fdca6-77da-5dd9-9d65-02c5cce92a93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d6689df-3f70-5bf3-89ff-1a1c94f97ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-149457" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,9].$\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $4\\sqrt{5}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{7}$ |\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-1}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $4\\sqrt{5}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $3\\sqrt{7}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(3)+g(5)+g(7)+g(9))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1+2+1+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5816063a-c3bd-5225-832f-fc4715f51f46"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c39ffa-cc37-57c6-bc37-b81b2034565a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503084} {:answer/id #uuid "53464bdb-2c4a-5641-a9f5-c6b77c691313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503140} {:answer/id #uuid "d746c8a3-bb8e-5572-8c8a-21e03e183d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503407} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1dcb43-72f9-5632-8b87-c67d9b670e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503468} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe7699b-53ea-50a7-b00d-672ab6f86d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503513} :answer-field/id #uuid "62861340-2e7e-599e-813a-37a384aba64c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ef423eeb-a6a9-5a03-bbd4-f26b7d9971da" :question/math-academy-id "q-4740" :question/problem "${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nWe see that if we let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(2).$  \n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{f}^{′}(2)={e}^{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude tha $L={e}^{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "80722edb-3694-511c-81f8-5d19307676e0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c9870f-0506-5064-ac23-b292234d4b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503057} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc73b7a-ef3d-547d-a954-d41ff011c35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503064} {:answer/id #uuid "34bb0305-64c5-5b32-bbd0-485d92cf9acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503076} {:answer/id #uuid "38b73058-6d97-5937-90c5-70fa98985408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503083} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fcce4d-e5c7-52f8-9d54-5a89dffa0fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503083} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa09ee35-3713-5a66-963f-92ef86cb648a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d3161c6d-15b1-5502-a086-55781c56e488" :question/math-academy-id "q-38592" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x-1}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2x-k, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\frac{k}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}(2x-k) \\\\  & =2(-1)-k \\\\  & =-2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-1)=-2-k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ -2-k & =-\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ -4-2k & =-k \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-4$ removes the discontinuity at $x=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "84264513-3dbb-52ef-bafc-b400f37e540e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a1370b-1edb-5c5a-a24f-eaa2993f5356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502985} {:answer/id #uuid "21bd116c-2d24-5d0e-ba67-f4580b818141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503045} {:answer/id #uuid "39083e26-3ab5-5681-8437-cc9ab5ac6173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503087} {:answer/id #uuid "9f14e973-894a-5a1a-8675-1eeeec064de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503300} {:answer/id #uuid "afd60b49-163e-5f97-8d94-74dba8c40864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503087} :answer-field/id #uuid "5109d536-d5f1-5dc6-99e6-dbfe65babe4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f20a6552-65e3-58b7-b9da-e6bf558e0b66" :question/math-academy-id "q-152889" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ec02a47-a1f9-5202-af32-d7dfa51ce29f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e670a4e-c1d6-5a75-a485-a130dfb0dd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b5cdb9170fbf8521bddc184d7a58cf0666ed9ad7d6323407ba1f518c59a9ede9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503066} {:answer/id #uuid "38b71195-e7d1-54ed-96b8-6998a9d3fd62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/08/0836910fb50c56022a624a3c98701d8f818c20246e0c6b368543414d8fdc388e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503082} {:answer/id #uuid "8b25af1e-9e5d-5a85-9cce-824bd044b2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/24/242c86c451468306416606ac87e8e1af324d5290b981a530c0fe671cd84603da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503261} {:answer/id #uuid "99251cbf-8178-51ff-96b3-c3adf56969e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d7/d714a50743775c00808537d0174d95d7abe59da6572ee339a6af8fae273aaafd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503284} {:answer/id #uuid "fd89e3b3-68d8-5eee-acba-d1d3b7eb731c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/43/43c320f61a34676966926f5757399330258b361c981f2aea2802a55dd6713760.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503066} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d76c14-c77d-5f6f-b333-af9e5bb2acd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ea6bbbd-4b7d-5900-9086-5360a21a6636" :question/math-academy-id "q-79412" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cff4fddd4ae530a233a4f667b3fc84cc2109914a59dda332cbcc8bd5838a516.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$\n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are negative and positive, respectively. \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5cdb9170fbf8521bddc184d7a58cf0666ed9ad7d6323407ba1f518c59a9ede9.png)"} :step/id #uuid "980e28b7-d752-5705-8bc8-64f1ee1bfe35"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddda751-0d34-586d-b038-e0a01f2febc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{73}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503037} {:answer/id #uuid "59169e9d-9262-57e2-9d1d-79d1fb530ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{89}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503153} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd61015-89c7-5df8-8f7f-0199795bc4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503161} {:answer/id #uuid "6892d97d-bead-5f58-8dab-7c42339644ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{111}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503185} {:answer/id #uuid "f65ed173-c910-51ea-9f87-39503a1ad9ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{91}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503037} :answer-field/id #uuid "c29dba39-a035-580c-9c90-3ddf062b8c8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "82a6bab7-1298-5d9a-9389-41a0c015118d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48021" :question/problem "Approximate the value of $\\sqrt{37}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=36.$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=36,$ which gives\n\n$$\nL(x)=f(36)+{f}^{′}(36)(x-36).\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(36)$ and ${f}^{′}(36)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf(36)=\\sqrt{36}=6,\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(36)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{36}}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x)=6+\\frac{1}{12}(x-36).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{37}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=37.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{37} & ≈L(37) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12}(37-36) \\\\  & =6+\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{73}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a970ca44-dc15-5295-9368-32779e3cef2a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0689c971-937c-53b8-b250-4c733f3231e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502994} {:answer/id #uuid "2b12c9f3-2819-529b-8837-45ccdf607dd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503062} {:answer/id #uuid "acddc305-2667-5de3-9e0a-d50c79f03f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503328} {:answer/id #uuid "b279ea53-ddd1-5ef6-943b-3673f2d4a39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503344} {:answer/id #uuid "f9efdb0d-9921-564c-9406-de62171a83e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503062} :answer-field/id #uuid "60159877-402e-50a4-b5a2-d2689fd74444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa4f2b60-2a66-5f4f-a213-69e38a19fcc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47501" :question/problem "Anna decides to go jogging. Her position from the starting point, measured in meters, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $14$ | $25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=7$ using a central difference approximation with the step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t+h)-s(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=7$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(7) & ≈\\frac{s(7+2)-s(7-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(9)-s(5)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{14-4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the velocity of Anna at the instant of time $t=7$ is $2.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "aa0baabe-b62f-5161-97a3-31b8b5541fe2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "560e7f3e-0d28-5092-9dc8-f284fb849140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503147} {:answer/id #uuid "96918186-4f3f-5672-8772-478e2533d76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503278} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d646eb-777f-5cfa-a5f5-fbdbfeb38a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+1){sinh}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503310} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7a5e34-b197-57ec-bafb-007ac6b6c167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{4}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503389} {:answer/id #uuid "da810825-b5ef-5b80-8415-5e95017d7de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503419} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfa9a94-c719-5199-ba06-985601fc058f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "828244f0-37d1-5218-b4cd-65ea8c882592" :question/math-academy-id "q-95329" :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x)=\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $g(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={sinh}^{2}⁡x$ and $v(x)=2x+1.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sinh}^{2}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2x+1)^{′}}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\cdot (2x+1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (2)}{(2x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b729a9f9-b3dd-56b1-a9d1-10bbdfa97f37"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050f0089-3abb-5b33-a522-9fcf6da51184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247462} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7ba62d-655c-5d2f-9e39-d57cfcb4fd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247536} {:answer/id #uuid "967f4525-a652-56db-858b-313b2768da07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{9+\\frac{4k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248762} {:answer/id #uuid "9e70dee7-9dc6-5645-af86-ccdb7c1fd656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248830} {:answer/id #uuid "c19dabf5-82d5-51cb-a5a0-053c1d283106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed8515fc-083c-5484-a9e2-8b6570186b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de045223-79af-5400-bb3f-2538c755ef59" :question/math-academy-id "q-113941" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c919d635-0450-5661-b44e-142fe0fbd91a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430337f1-4c86-50c2-883d-98f137d23325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.5\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503109} {:answer/id #uuid "648432b2-e9e4-5a70-b9af-d8b7b570d17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.25\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503177} {:answer/id #uuid "931f54e7-f77f-56ee-ade7-8ea1f562b8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.5\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503273} {:answer/id #uuid "db253da5-f871-5e99-be8b-c740057241e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503420} {:answer/id #uuid "f9406b8a-7827-5235-b885-3b2359724270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\text{ cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503109} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb2aeff-2de4-5697-9623-6df504a488b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2d45e40-2970-570b-ba18-c7ae81412efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-116735" :question/problem "![](images/63/63fe9cf3a61a5cef09f9c19e16a18318338faa6837eeb77b83f49b636157bb95.png)\n\nA sphere is decreasing in volume at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ Calculate the rate at which the radius of the sphere is decreasing at the moment when the radius is $3\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-54\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-54\\pi $ and $r=3,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-54\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\ -54\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 36\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the sphere is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\text{ cm/s}$ when the radius is $3\\,\\text{cm}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d6664ed0-cc8a-5a48-84c2-9c9ed0027f97"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16834030-119c-50b4-9662-edd5f91fc424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503027} {:answer/id #uuid "565b2c1d-8909-5088-ae15-f6d4e0aa6c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503149} {:answer/id #uuid "63422371-897b-542b-9d8a-828fa718a920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503173} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ef85bf-0c33-54a7-91ff-75cb78a92187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503404} {:answer/id #uuid "f24c9e76-cd98-5133-8f17-177d55d12f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503027} :answer-field/id #uuid "488d2faf-a1cd-5796-984d-787dccc3cd23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bf7b7ef5-50f5-5f18-b878-7ff1f9484b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-73492" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-2,-1]$\n\nII. $[-1,1]$\n\nIII. $(0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-2,-1].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $(0,1]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "db65a67e-6455-5feb-9447-703b78408667"}] :activity/title "Calculus I: Placement Exam" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045462 :db/ident :activity.type/diagnostic} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502966}]}
